1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:08,000
1.1 billion parameters. One ARM chip. One command.
Watch the pipeline think.

2
00:00:16,500 --> 00:00:24,500
Most LLMs live in cloud GPUs that six billion people
will never touch. TinyLLM-ARM-Pro makes inference run
on the Arm64 chips people actually have.

3
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:35,000
The speed is engineered at every layer: K-quant Q4_K_M
chosen by evidence, llama.cpp built from source for Arm64,
Metal offload — and kernels written by hand.

4
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:45,500
Four quantization levels. One verdict: Q4_K_M.
Fastest and most accurate — on two model families,
two Arm64 machines.

5
00:00:45,800 --> 00:00:52,500
At the bottom we speak the chip's language — 128-bit
NEON registers, four lanes of fused multiply-add.

6
00:00:53,000 --> 00:01:00,000
I8MM SMMLA eight-bit dot products — the same instruction
family Arm ships in its Compute Library.
Our kernels: 12.5x faster than scalar.

7
00:01:00,500 --> 00:01:07,500
Flash Attention prefill: 1,329 tokens per second.

8
00:01:08,500 --> 00:01:18,500
Quantization is a science, not a hack. Academic WikiText-2:
8.73 perplexity against the community's 8.74 — the same
quality at 5.75x the speed, in 83% less memory.

9
00:01:19,500 --> 00:01:27,500
The same pipeline ran on two Arm64 machines — this M4,
and a Cobalt 100 cloud instance. Same commands. Same verdict.
Write once, run on any Arm64.

10
00:01:28,500 --> 00:01:36,500
The planner predicts performance before a single token,
then validates live and publishes the honest error:
3.4 percent, in the real run.

11
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:49,000
TinyLLM-ARM-Pro. Open source. MIT licensed.
Every number reproducible with one command.
LLM inference for the other six billion.